  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/wertheimeria-maculata-2/",
      "html": "<p>Wertheimeria maculata belongs to the strangest thorny catfishes. Adults are about 30 cm long and look spectacular: on a black background the species has large, cream-colored spots.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/wertheimeria-maculata-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species is endemic and occurs exclusively in the Jequitinhonha and Pardo rivers in Brazil. The area of occurrence covers more than twice the area of Switzerland, and W. maculata is not considered endangered.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/wertheimeria-maculata-2/",
      "html": "<p>They have no special demands on water, but it goes without saying that they should be kept in aquariums as large as possible with well maintained water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/wertheimeria-maculata-2/",
      "html": "<p>Wertheimeria maculata do not have special demands on food, but they should be fed with high quality food (mussel meat is ideal, but any commercially available ornamental fish food is accepted).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/wertheimeria-maculata-2/",
      "html": "<p>Whoever acquires this species should keep it for himself if possible. Young animals are compatible with each other. The animals are hiding-loving and mainly nocturnal and are easily disturbed by other species. One hardly gets to see them then.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/wertheimeria-maculata-2/",
      "html": "<p>The genus Wertheimeria was established for the species at its first description in 1877 and it remains the only member to this day. Within the spiny catfishes (Doradidae) a separate subfamily Wertheimerinae was established for the species and two others (Franciscodoras marmoratus and Kalyptodoras bahiensis).</p>"
    }
  ],
